      "output": "At its simplest, **water** is the most abundant and essential molecule for life on Earth. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it from scientific, environmental, and biological perspectives:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Chemical Composition\nChemically, water is a compound made of two types of atoms: **Hydrogen** and **Oxygen**. \n* Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**. \n* This means every single molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n\n### 2. Physical Properties\nWater is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n* **Liquid:** The standard form we drink, swim in, and find in oceans and rivers.\n* **Solid (Ice):** Formed when water freezes at or below 0°C (32°F). Unlike most substances, water *expands* and becomes less dense when it freezes, which is why ice floats.\n* **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Formed when water boils at 100°C (212°F) or evaporates into the air.\n\nWater is also known as the **\"Universal Solvent\"** because it can dissolve more substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals) than any other liquid. This ability is vital for chemical and biological processes.\n\n### 3. Earth's Lifeline\nWater covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). \n* Only about **2.5% is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. \n* Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use in lakes, rivers, and streams.\n\n### 4. Biological Importance\nWater is the fundamental building block of life. \n* The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**.\n* It acts as a transport system, carrying nutrients and oxygen to cells, regulating body temperature, flushing out waste, and cushioning joints and organs.\n* Without water, plants cannot perform photosynthesis, meaning the entire global food chain would collapse.\n\nIn summary, water is a simple chemical molecule that is miraculous in its properties, acting as the driving force of weather, geology, and life itself."
